import Mathlib

/- A–M assume throughout that the index set `I` is *directed*.  That hypothesis is carried in
every statement of the `Limit` section for faithfulness to the exercise, although the
constructions of Exercises 14–18 never consume it (only Exercise 15 does).  The linter that
would flag it as unused is therefore disabled here. -/
set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false

/-!
# Atiyah–Macdonald (1969), Chapter 2, Exercise 18

## The statement of the exercise

(Atiyah–Macdonald, *Introduction to Commutative Algebra*, Chapter 2, Exercise 18.)

Let `M = (Mᵢ, μᵢⱼ)`, `N = (Nᵢ, νᵢⱼ)` be direct systems of `A`-modules over the same directed
set `I` (Exercise 14).  Let `M`, `N` be the direct limits and `μᵢ : Mᵢ → M`, `νᵢ : Nᵢ → N` the
associated homomorphisms.  A *homomorphism* `Φ : M → N` is by definition a family of
`A`-module homomorphisms `φᵢ : Mᵢ → Nᵢ` such that `φⱼ ∘ μᵢⱼ = νᵢⱼ ∘ φᵢ` whenever `i ≤ j`.
Show that `Φ` defines a unique homomorphism `φ = lim Φ : M → N` such that
`φ ∘ μᵢ = νᵢ ∘ φᵢ` for all `i ∈ I`.

## What is formalized

* `IsDirectSystem μ` — axioms (1) and (2) of Exercise 14 for the transition maps `μᵢⱼ`.
* `Module.DirectLimit M μ` — the direct limit, i.e. A–M's construction: the quotient of the
  direct sum `C = ⨁ᵢ Mᵢ` by the submodule `D` generated by the elements `xᵢ - μᵢⱼ xᵢ`.  Writing
  down the direct sum needs a decidable equality on `I`; that is supplied by `Classical.decEq`
  inside this file and is therefore *not* a hypothesis of anything below.
  Since the direct sum over an empty index set is the zero module, this construction also gives
  the correct direct limit (zero) when `I` is empty — unlike the quotient of the disjoint union,
  which is empty in that case.  Consequently **no** nonemptiness of `I` is assumed anywhere in
  the main results, and the empty case is covered too (`existsUnique_limMap_of_isEmpty`,
  `subsingleton_directLimit_of_isEmpty`).
* `limOf μ i : M i →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit M μ` — the canonical map `μᵢ : Mᵢ → lim M`.
* `limOf_of_le` — `μⱼ ∘ μᵢⱼ = μᵢ` (functoriality of the system, Exercise 14).
* `exists_limOf` — Exercise 15: every element of the limit is `μᵢ xᵢ` for some `i`, `xᵢ`
  (this is the one statement that does need `I` nonempty, and does assume it).
* `existsUnique_lift` — Exercise 16, the universal property of the direct limit.  It is the
  result the natural solution of Exercise 18 appeals to.
* `Hom μ ν` — a homomorphism of direct systems (the `Φ` of the exercise).
* `Hom.limMap Φ` — `φ = lim Φ`; `Hom.limMap_comp_limOf` — `φ ∘ μᵢ = νᵢ ∘ φᵢ`;
  `Hom.limMap_unique`, `Hom.existsUnique_limMap` — uniqueness, in the exact form asked for.
  No hypothesis on `I` beyond `Preorder I` and `IsDirectedOrder I`.
* `Hom.limMap_id`, `Hom.limMap_comp` — `lim` is a functor.
* `existsUnique_limMap_of_universal` — the same result for abstract cones, which records that
  the natural proof uses nothing but Exercise 16.
* `not_exists_limMap_of_not_natural` — the naturality hypothesis `Hom.comm` cannot be dropped.

## On the natural proof

The source solution reads: *"The maps `νᵢ φᵢ` are compatible, since
`νⱼ φⱼ μᵢⱼ = νⱼ νᵢⱼ φᵢ = νᵢ φᵢ`. Apply 2.16. The resulting map is
`φ(μᵢ(x)) = νᵢ(φᵢ(x))`."*

That is the complete argument, but it suppresses three obligations, all discharged below:
(i) the compatibility hypothesis of Exercise 16 must be checked for the specific family
`gᵢ = νᵢ ∘ φᵢ` (this is `limMapFamily_compat`, where it is exactly the interchange
`νⱼ ∘ (φⱼ ∘ μᵢⱼ) = (νⱼ ∘ νᵢⱼ) ∘ φᵢ`, using `φⱼ ∘ μᵢⱼ = νᵢⱼ ∘ φᵢ` and `νᵢ = νⱼ ∘ νᵢⱼ`);
(ii) *both* halves of Exercise 16 are used — existence gives `φ`, and uniqueness of the map out
of `lim M` gives the uniqueness of `φ` (it is not a separate argument);
(iii) the direction of the naturality condition matters: it is `φⱼ ∘ μᵢⱼ = νᵢⱼ ∘ φᵢ`, not the
other way round, and the shape of `Hom.comm` below records it in that direction.

The empty index set needs no separate argument for the *limit* itself (the construction gives
`0 ⧸ 0 = 0`); the two lemmas at the end of the `Limit` section record it explicitly.
-/

namespace AtiyahMcdonald69.Ex_2_18

/-- A direct system of `A`-modules over a preordered index set `I`: axioms (1) and (2) of
Atiyah–Macdonald, Chapter 2, Exercise 14, for the transition maps
`μ i j h : M i →ₗ[A] M j` (`h : i ≤ j`). -/
class IsDirectSystem {A : Type*} [CommRing A] {I : Type*} [Preorder I] {M : I → Type*}
    [∀ i, AddCommGroup (M i)] [∀ i, Module A (M i)]
    (μ : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → M i →ₗ[A] M j) : Prop where
  /-- Axiom (1): `μᵢᵢ` is the identity. -/
  map_self : ∀ i x, μ i i le_rfl x = x
  /-- Axiom (2): `μᵢₖ = μⱼₖ ∘ μᵢⱼ` for `i ≤ j ≤ k`. -/
  map_map : ∀ i j k (hij : i ≤ j) (hjk : j ≤ k) (x : M i),
    μ j k hjk (μ i j hij x) = μ i k (hij.trans hjk) x

section Bare

variable {A : Type*} [CommRing A]
variable {I : Type*} [Preorder I]
variable {M N P : I → Type*}
variable [∀ i, AddCommGroup (M i)] [∀ i, Module A (M i)]
variable [∀ i, AddCommGroup (N i)] [∀ i, Module A (N i)]
variable [∀ i, AddCommGroup (P i)] [∀ i, Module A (P i)]

/-! ### Homomorphisms of direct systems (the `Φ` of Exercise 18) -/

/-- A homomorphism `Φ : M → N` of direct systems over the same index set: a family of
`A`-module homomorphisms `φᵢ : Mᵢ → Nᵢ` with `φⱼ ∘ μᵢⱼ = νᵢⱼ ∘ φᵢ` for `i ≤ j`
(Atiyah–Macdonald, Chapter 2, Exercise 18). -/
structure Hom (μ : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → M i →ₗ[A] M j) (ν : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → N i →ₗ[A] N j) where
  /-- The component maps `φᵢ`. -/
  map : ∀ i, M i →ₗ[A] N i
  /-- Naturality: `φⱼ ∘ μᵢⱼ = νᵢⱼ ∘ φᵢ` for `i ≤ j`. -/
  comm : ∀ i j (h : i ≤ j), (ν i j h).comp (map i) = (map j).comp (μ i j h)

namespace Hom

@[ext]
theorem ext {μ : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → M i →ₗ[A] M j} {ν : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → N i →ₗ[A] N j}
    {Φ Ψ : Hom μ ν} (h : ∀ i, Φ.map i = Ψ.map i) : Φ = Ψ := by
  obtain ⟨f, hf⟩ := Φ
  obtain ⟨g, hg⟩ := Ψ
  obtain rfl : f = g := funext h
  exact congrArg (Hom.mk (μ := μ) (ν := ν) f) (Subsingleton.elim hf hg)

/-- The identity homomorphism of a direct system. -/
protected def id (μ : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → M i →ₗ[A] M j) : Hom μ μ where
  map _ := LinearMap.id
  comm _ _ _ := by simp

/-- Composition of homomorphisms of direct systems. -/
protected def comp {μ : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → M i →ₗ[A] M j} {ν : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → N i →ₗ[A] N j}
    {π : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → P i →ₗ[A] P j} (Ψ : Hom ν π) (Φ : Hom μ ν) : Hom μ π where
  map i := (Ψ.map i).comp (Φ.map i)
  comm i j h := by
    ext x
    have key₁ : π i j h (Ψ.map i (Φ.map i x)) = Ψ.map j (ν i j h (Φ.map i x)) :=
      LinearMap.congr_fun (Ψ.comm i j h) (Φ.map i x)
    have key₂ : ν i j h (Φ.map i x) = Φ.map j (μ i j h x) :=
      LinearMap.congr_fun (Φ.comm i j h) x
    simp only [LinearMap.comp_apply]
    rw [key₁, key₂]

end Hom

end Bare

section Limit

variable {A : Type*} [CommRing A]
variable {I : Type*} [Preorder I] [IsDirectedOrder I]
variable {M N P : I → Type*}
variable [∀ i, AddCommGroup (M i)] [∀ i, Module A (M i)]
variable [∀ i, AddCommGroup (N i)] [∀ i, Module A (N i)]
variable [∀ i, AddCommGroup (P i)] [∀ i, Module A (P i)]
variable {μ : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → M i →ₗ[A] M j} [IsDirectSystem μ]
variable {ν : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → N i →ₗ[A] N j} [IsDirectSystem ν]
variable {π : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → P i →ₗ[A] P j} [IsDirectSystem π]

noncomputable section

/-- `Module.DirectLimit M μ` is the direct limit of Exercise 14: the quotient of the direct sum
`⨁ᵢ Mᵢ` by the submodule generated by the elements `xᵢ - μᵢⱼ xᵢ`.  Writing down the direct sum
requires a decidable equality on `I`; we supply it classically, so it is not a hypothesis of any
statement below (and in particular not a mathematical assumption).  Unlike the quotient of the
disjoint union, this construction is also correct for the empty index set, where the direct sum
and hence the limit is the zero module. -/
local instance : DecidableEq I := Classical.decEq I

/-- The axioms of a direct system, fed to Mathlib's `DirectedSystem` class. -/
instance directedSystemOfIsDirectSystem (μ : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → M i →ₗ[A] M j) [IsDirectSystem μ] :
    DirectedSystem M (fun i j h => μ i j h) where
  map_self := fun {i} x => IsDirectSystem.map_self (μ := μ) i x
  map_map := fun {k j i} hij hjk x => IsDirectSystem.map_map (μ := μ) i j k hij hjk x

/-! ### The direct limit and its canonical maps -/

/-- `μᵢ : Mᵢ → lim M`, the canonical map from a component of the direct system to the direct
limit (Atiyah–Macdonald, Chapter 2, Exercise 14). -/
abbrev limOf (μ : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → M i →ₗ[A] M j) [IsDirectSystem μ] (i : I) :
    M i →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit M μ :=
  Module.DirectLimit.of A I M μ i

omit [IsDirectedOrder I] in
/-- The defining relation of the direct limit: `μᵢ = μⱼ ∘ μᵢⱼ` for `i ≤ j`. -/
theorem limOf_of_le (i j : I) (h : i ≤ j) (x : M i) :
    limOf μ j (μ i j h x) = limOf μ i x :=
  Module.DirectLimit.of_f

/-- The same relation between the linear maps themselves. -/
theorem limOf_comp (i j : I) (h : i ≤ j) :
    (limOf μ j).comp (μ i j h) = limOf μ i :=
  LinearMap.ext fun x => limOf_of_le i j h x

omit [IsDirectedOrder I] in
/-- Two maps out of the direct limit agree as soon as they agree after composing with every
canonical map `μᵢ`. -/
theorem lim_ext {Q : Type*} [AddCommGroup Q] [Module A Q]
    {F G : Module.DirectLimit M μ →ₗ[A] Q} (h : ∀ i : I, F ∘ₗ limOf μ i = G ∘ₗ limOf μ i) :
    F = G :=
  Module.DirectLimit.hom_ext h

/-- Atiyah–Macdonald, Chapter 2, Exercise 15: every element of the direct limit is the image of
an element of one of the components.  (This is the one statement here that needs `I` nonempty:
over an empty index set the limit is `0` and has no such representation.) -/
theorem exists_limOf [Nonempty I] (z : Module.DirectLimit M μ) :
    ∃ (i : I) (x : M i), limOf μ i x = z :=
  Module.DirectLimit.exists_of z

/-! ### Exercise 16: the universal property of the direct limit -/

/-- Atiyah–Macdonald, Chapter 2, Exercise 16: a compatible family of maps out of the components
of a direct system induces a unique map out of the direct limit. -/
theorem existsUnique_lift {Q : Type*} [AddCommGroup Q] [Module A Q] (g : ∀ i, M i →ₗ[A] Q)
    (hg : ∀ i j (h : i ≤ j) (x : M i), g j (μ i j h x) = g i x) :
    ∃! F : Module.DirectLimit M μ →ₗ[A] Q, ∀ i, F ∘ₗ limOf μ i = g i := by
  refine ⟨Module.DirectLimit.lift A I M μ g hg, fun i => ?_, fun F hF => ?_⟩
  · exact LinearMap.ext fun x => Module.DirectLimit.lift_of g hg x
  · refine lim_ext fun i => ?_
    rw [hF i]
    exact (LinearMap.ext fun x => Module.DirectLimit.lift_of g hg x).symm

/-! ### Exercise 18: the map on direct limits induced by a homomorphism of systems -/

namespace Hom

/-- The family of maps `gᵢ = νᵢ ∘ φᵢ` out of the components of the first system.  The natural
solution of Exercise 18 checks that it is compatible and applies Exercise 16 to it. -/
def limMapFamily (Φ : Hom μ ν) (i : I) : M i →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit N ν :=
  (limOf ν i).comp (Φ.map i)

omit [IsDirectSystem μ] in
/-- Compatibility of the family `gᵢ = νᵢ ∘ φᵢ`, which is the hypothesis of Exercise 16:
`νⱼ φⱼ μᵢⱼ = νⱼ νᵢⱼ φᵢ = νᵢ φᵢ`. -/
theorem limMapFamily_compat (Φ : Hom μ ν) :
    ∀ i j (h : i ≤ j) (x : M i), limMapFamily Φ j (μ i j h x) = limMapFamily Φ i x := by
  intro i j h x
  have key : ν i j h (Φ.map i x) = Φ.map j (μ i j h x) := LinearMap.congr_fun (Φ.comm i j h) x
  simp only [limMapFamily, LinearMap.comp_apply]
  rw [← key, limOf_of_le]

/-- Atiyah–Macdonald, Chapter 2, Exercise 18: the homomorphism `φ = lim Φ : lim M → lim N`
induced by a homomorphism `Φ : M → N` of direct systems. -/
def limMap (Φ : Hom μ ν) : Module.DirectLimit M μ →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit N ν :=
  Module.DirectLimit.lift A I M μ (limMapFamily Φ) (limMapFamily_compat Φ)

/-- The defining property of the induced map, pointwise: `φ (μᵢ x) = νᵢ (φᵢ x)`. -/
theorem limMap_of (Φ : Hom μ ν) (i : I) (x : M i) :
    limMap Φ (limOf μ i x) = limOf ν i (Φ.map i x) :=
  Module.DirectLimit.lift_of (limMapFamily Φ) (limMapFamily_compat Φ) x

/-- The defining property of the induced map, in the form `φ ∘ μᵢ = νᵢ ∘ φᵢ`. -/
theorem limMap_comp_limOf (Φ : Hom μ ν) (i : I) :
    (limMap Φ).comp (limOf μ i) = (limOf ν i).comp (Φ.map i) :=
  LinearMap.ext (limMap_of Φ i)

/-- Uniqueness in Exercise 18: any `A`-linear map out of `lim M` with the required property
coincides with `lim Φ`. -/
theorem limMap_unique (Φ : Hom μ ν) (F : Module.DirectLimit M μ →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit N ν)
    (hF : ∀ i, F ∘ₗ limOf μ i = (limOf ν i).comp (Φ.map i)) : F = limMap Φ := by
  refine lim_ext fun i => ?_
  rw [hF i]
  exact (limMap_comp_limOf Φ i).symm

/-- Uniqueness in Exercise 18, pointwise form. -/
theorem limMap_unique_apply (Φ : Hom μ ν) (F : Module.DirectLimit M μ →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit N ν)
    (hF : ∀ (i : I) (x : M i), F (limOf μ i x) = limOf ν i (Φ.map i x)) : F = limMap Φ :=
  limMap_unique Φ F fun i => LinearMap.ext (hF i)

/-- Exercise 18, in the exact form of the exercise: `Φ` defines a unique homomorphism
`φ = lim Φ : lim M → lim N` such that `φ ∘ μᵢ = νᵢ ∘ φᵢ` for all `i ∈ I`.  No hypothesis on the
index set beyond `Preorder I` and `IsDirectedOrder I` is used — in particular `I` may be empty. -/
theorem existsUnique_limMap (Φ : Hom μ ν) :
    ∃! F : Module.DirectLimit M μ →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit N ν,
      ∀ i : I, F ∘ₗ limOf μ i = (limOf ν i).comp (Φ.map i) :=
  ⟨limMap Φ, limMap_comp_limOf Φ, fun _ hF => limMap_unique Φ _ hF⟩

/-- Exercise 18, pointwise form of the uniqueness statement. -/
theorem existsUnique_limMap_apply (Φ : Hom μ ν) :
    ∃! F : Module.DirectLimit M μ →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit N ν,
      ∀ (i : I) (x : M i), F (limOf μ i x) = limOf ν i (Φ.map i x) := by
  obtain ⟨F, hF, huniq⟩ := existsUnique_limMap Φ
  exact ⟨F, fun i x => LinearMap.congr_fun (hF i) x, fun G hG => huniq G fun i => LinearMap.ext (hG i)⟩

/-! ### `lim` is a functor -/

/-- `lim` of the identity homomorphism is the identity. -/
theorem limMap_id : limMap (Hom.id μ) = LinearMap.id := by
  refine lim_ext fun i => ?_
  ext x
  rw [LinearMap.comp_apply, LinearMap.comp_apply, limMap_of, LinearMap.id_apply]
  rfl

/-- `lim` of a composite is the composite of the induced maps. -/
theorem limMap_comp (Ψ : Hom ν π) (Φ : Hom μ ν) :
    (limMap Ψ).comp (limMap Φ) = limMap (Ψ.comp Φ) := by
  refine lim_ext fun i => ?_
  ext x
  simp only [LinearMap.comp_apply, limMap_of]
  rfl

end Hom

/-! ### The empty index set

A–M's definition of a directed set does not exclude the empty one, and the construction above
gives the correct (zero) direct limit in that case: the direct sum over `∅` is `0`.  The two
results below record that Exercise 18 therefore holds there as well, with the zero map as the
unique map. -/

/-- Over the empty index set the direct limit is the zero module: the identity and the zero
endomorphism of the limit agree, because `lim_ext` has no index at which to test them. -/
theorem subsingleton_directLimit_of_isEmpty [IsEmpty I] : Subsingleton (Module.DirectLimit M μ) := by
  have h : (LinearMap.id : Module.DirectLimit M μ →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit M μ) = 0 := by
    refine lim_ext fun i => (IsEmpty.false i).elim
  constructor
  intro a b
  have ha : a = 0 := by
    have h1 := LinearMap.congr_fun h a
    rwa [LinearMap.id_apply, LinearMap.zero_apply] at h1
  have hb : b = 0 := by
    have h1 := LinearMap.congr_fun h b
    rwa [LinearMap.id_apply, LinearMap.zero_apply] at h1
  rw [ha, hb]

/-- Exercise 18 for the empty index set: both limits are the zero module and the family of
equations is empty, so there is a unique (the zero) map between them. -/
theorem existsUnique_limMap_of_isEmpty [IsEmpty I] (Φ : Hom μ ν) :
    ∃! F : Module.DirectLimit M μ →ₗ[A] Module.DirectLimit N ν,
      ∀ i : I, F ∘ₗ limOf μ i = (limOf ν i).comp (Φ.map i) :=
  Hom.existsUnique_limMap Φ

end

end Limit

/-! ### Exercise 18 for an abstract pair of direct limits

The natural solution argues: the maps `νᵢ ∘ φᵢ` are compatible, so Exercise 16 produces `φ`.
That argument uses nothing about `lim M` and `lim N` beyond the universal property of `lim M`
and the compatibility of the maps `νᵢ`, so we record it in that generality. -/

section Abstract

universe u

variable {A : Type*} [CommRing A]
variable {I : Type*} [Preorder I]
variable {M N : I → Type*}
variable [∀ i, AddCommGroup (M i)] [∀ i, Module A (M i)]
variable [∀ i, AddCommGroup (N i)] [∀ i, Module A (N i)]

/-- Exercise 18 for abstract cones: if `mᵢ : Mᵢ → LM` satisfies the universal property of
Exercise 16 and `nᵢ : Nᵢ → LN` is compatible with the transition maps of `N`, then every
homomorphism `Φ` of direct systems induces a unique `F : LM → LN` with `F ∘ mᵢ = nᵢ ∘ φᵢ`.
Taking `LM = lim M`, `m = limOf μ`, `LN = lim N`, `n = limOf ν` recovers Exercise 18, where
`hn` is `limOf_of_le` and `hU` is `existsUnique_lift`. -/
theorem existsUnique_limMap_of_universal {LM LN : Type u} [AddCommGroup LM] [Module A LM]
    [AddCommGroup LN] [Module A LN] (μ : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → M i →ₗ[A] M j)
    (ν : ∀ i j, i ≤ j → N i →ₗ[A] N j) (m : ∀ i, M i →ₗ[A] LM) (n : ∀ i, N i →ₗ[A] LN)
    (hn : ∀ i j (h : i ≤ j), (n j).comp (ν i j h) = n i)
    (hU : ∀ {Q : Type u} [AddCommGroup Q] [Module A Q] (g : ∀ i, M i →ₗ[A] Q),
      (∀ i j (h : i ≤ j), (g j).comp (μ i j h) = g i) → ∃! F : LM →ₗ[A] Q, ∀ i, F ∘ₗ m i = g i)
    (Φ : Hom μ ν) :
    ∃! F : LM →ₗ[A] LN, ∀ i, F ∘ₗ m i = (n i).comp (Φ.map i) := by
  refine hU (Q := LN) (fun i => (n i).comp (Φ.map i)) ?_
  intro i j h
  show ((n j).comp (Φ.map j)).comp (μ i j h) = (n i).comp (Φ.map i)
  rw [LinearMap.comp_assoc, ← Φ.comm i j h, ← LinearMap.comp_assoc, hn i j h]

end Abstract

/-! ### Non-vacuity of the hypotheses, and the naturality hypothesis is not redundant -/

section Example

noncomputable section

local instance : DecidableEq ℕ := Classical.decEq ℕ

/-- The transition maps of the identity direct system over `ℕ`. -/
def idTrans (A : Type*) [CommRing A] : ∀ i j : ℕ, i ≤ j → A →ₗ[A] A := fun _ _ _ => LinearMap.id

/-- The identity system over `ℕ` is a direct system of `A`-modules. -/
instance isDirectSystemIdTrans (A : Type*) [CommRing A] :
    IsDirectSystem (A := A) (I := ℕ) (M := fun _ => A) (idTrans A) where
  map_self _ _ := rfl
  map_map _ _ _ _ _ _ := rfl

instance isDirectedOrderNat : IsDirectedOrder ℕ :=
  ⟨fun a b => ⟨max a b, le_max_left a b, le_max_right a b⟩⟩

/-- The direct limit of the identity system of `A` over `ℕ`. -/
noncomputable abbrev natLim (A : Type*) [CommRing A] : Type _ :=
  Module.DirectLimit (fun _ : ℕ => A) (idTrans A)

/-- The canonical retraction `lim A → A` of the identity system over `ℕ`. -/
noncomputable def natLimRetraction (A : Type*) [CommRing A] : natLim A →ₗ[A] A :=
  Module.DirectLimit.lift A ℕ (fun _ : ℕ => A) (idTrans A) (fun _ => LinearMap.id)
    (fun _ _ _ _ => rfl)

theorem natLimRetraction_apply (A : Type*) [CommRing A] (i : ℕ) (x : A) :
    natLimRetraction A (limOf (idTrans A) i x) = x :=
  Module.DirectLimit.lift_of (fun _ => (LinearMap.id : A →ₗ[A] A)) (fun _ _ _ _ => rfl) x

/-- The hypotheses of Exercise 18 are satisfiable: the identity homomorphism of the identity
system over `ℕ`, whose induced map is the identity of the direct limit. -/
example {A : Type*} [CommRing A] :
    (Hom.id (idTrans A)).limMap = LinearMap.id :=
  Hom.limMap_id

/-- The family `φᵢ = i • id` of the identity system over `ℕ` does not satisfy the naturality
condition `φⱼ ∘ μᵢⱼ = νᵢⱼ ∘ φᵢ`, and it satisfies the *conclusion* of Exercise 18 for no linear
map at all: the hypothesis `Hom.comm` cannot be dropped.  (Evaluating at `i = 0` and `i = 1`,
which have the same canonical image in `lim M`, would force `0 • 1 = 1 • 1`.) -/
theorem not_exists_limMap_of_not_natural (A : Type*) [CommRing A] [Nontrivial A] :
    ¬ ∃ F : natLim A →ₗ[A] natLim A,
      ∀ (i : ℕ) (x : A), F (limOf (idTrans A) i x) = limOf (idTrans A) i ((i : A) • x) := by
  rintro ⟨F, hF⟩
  -- `μ₀ = μ₁`: the two components have the same canonical image in the limit
  have h01 : ∀ x : A, limOf (idTrans A) (1 : ℕ) x = limOf (idTrans A) 0 x := fun x =>
    limOf_of_le (μ := idTrans A) (i := 0) (j := 1) (Nat.zero_le 1) x
  -- the two equations at `i = 1` and `i = 0`, read through the retraction, give `1 = 0`
  have hA : natLimRetraction A (F (limOf (idTrans A) 1 (1 : A))) = 1 := by
    rw [hF 1 1]
    simp only [Nat.cast_one, one_smul]
    exact natLimRetraction_apply A 1 1
  have hB : natLimRetraction A (F (limOf (idTrans A) 1 (1 : A))) = 0 := by
    rw [h01 1, hF 0 1]
    simp only [Nat.cast_zero, zero_smul]
    exact natLimRetraction_apply A 0 0
  exact one_ne_zero (hA.symm.trans hB)

end

end Example

